        "translation_uz": "caudofemoralis muscle (Suyak)",
        "definition_uz": "Anatomik ta'rif (Suyak): The caudofemoralis (from the latin cauda, tail and femur, thighbone) is a muscle found in the pelvic limb of mostly all animals possessing a tail, since it is a synapomorphy appeared on the Archosauria clade. It is thus found in felids (cats) and Mustela (&#x27;Weasels&#x27;), but also on crocodiles and birds.",
        "slug": "/structure/caudofemoralis/",
        "description_en": "The caudofemoralis (from the latin cauda, tail and femur, thighbone) is a muscle found in the pelvic limb of mostly all animals possessing a tail, since it is a synapomorphy appeared on the Archosauria clade. It is thus found in felids (cats) and Mustela (&#x27;Weasels&#x27;), but also on crocodiles and birds."
